<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arch of Titus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 81</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Arco di Tito, Arcus Titi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> is an honorific arch on the Via Sacra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, just south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a>, built in about 81 CE by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> after the death of his brother Titus. It commemorates Titus's deification and his victory, with their father Vespasian, over the Jewish rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>. Its panels show the triumphal procession of 71 CE after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, with spoils from Herod's Temple, including the menorah. The arch became a symbol of the Jewish diaspora, its menorah the model for the emblem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel">State of Israel</a>, and it inspired later triumphal arches, among them the Arc de Triomphe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arch-of-titus.jpg" alt="Arch of Titus" width="330" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jebulon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arch_Titus,_Forum_Romanum,_Rome,_Italy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
